# The protocol carries no requester uid/gid, so an object created by uid 65534 through the mount belongs to the server user (root here). Every check that expects 65534 as owner or group of what it created fails, and so does everything that depends on that ownership: chmod, chown, rename, unlink and link by 65534 on 'its own' files (EPERM or EACCES from the kernel's permission and sticky-directory checks and from the fs.protected_* sysctls), the opens that then succeed or fail against the creation mode because the chmod before them was refused, and the lstat that then finds the old name still there or the new one missing. (160 checks)
